[{:seon.task/name "the-binary-main-is-the-system-process"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Deps: the binary's main becomes the system process (store opened, root forked first, root's socket handed to it); the Jolt pin by which a forked child never collects what it inherited (its own proof, the fork-server and agent-fork tests run on the candidate, flat across merges measured); jolt.host/process-footprint-bytes made to read phys_footprint (it answers 818.7 MB where the platform says 107 MB, web result/ccaf33fb); trace the 174 MB a fresh agent writes at its start, to <=5 MB. STATUS 2026-11-16 (deps): Jolt pin READY on its own (jolt/deps 43dfd9d671; a child's collection frees 0 of what it inherited, was 27.3 MB; live heap flat across two program moves, 1.126 then 1.125 GB). process-footprint-bytes is NOT a defect: it equals the OS footprint in one process (730.6 vs 731 MB, result/aac89d96; web confirmed). A fresh agent's start-up writes 361-585 MB (phys_footprint), the open part. PLAN (deps, for root and runtime before code). PRIOR WORK read: seon.main/root-serve (the binary's root mode: c/start!, its stop on stdin EOF or a signal), seon.system/start! and boot! (opens the store, seeds or catches up, loads main's program, instruments, warms, then forks the fork server: system.clj ~1695-1880), seon.fork-server/fork-server, main and server-step (the loop over root's socket: :tx, :fork, :exit, :stats, standby and promote), seon.lib/fork!. THE SHAPE: (1) seon.main's root mode becomes the system process, one thread for life: compact-if-due! first (LMDB's alone open: nothing else holds the store yet); open the store and connect main READ-ONLY in use (no write: root is the one writer); raise gc-generations; hold main's config, load main's program (load-all! against the binary's), instrument the contracts, run the read-only warm paths; collect in full. (2) It binds 127.0.0.1:server-port once, before it forks root, keeps that fd for its life, and passes it to each root it forks as :seon.server/listen-fd in root's start map, kept across the fork (lib/fork!'s :keep); agents and test children close it by fork!'s default (agreed with runtime and web, 2026-11-16). Web builds serve! over it with jolt.host/server-socket-from-fd (task jolt.host/a-server-socket-is-made-from-an-inherited-fd, deps'). (3) It makes root's socketpair and forks ROOT FIRST (lib/fork!, one thread by construction). The child runs today's start! minus what it inherited: no program load, no instrument, no warm, no fork-server spawn; it does the store's writes (drop-trees, seed or catch-up, the boot fact, the writer, the api, the reconciler) and talks to its parent over that socket as it talks to the fork server today (:fork, :stats), sending every commit as a :tx notice (data's seon.writer/tx-notice, boot's commits included). (4) The parent then runs the fork server's loop (server-step) on that socket: :tx loads by load-diff! (runtime's arm), :fork forks an agent or test child (root's siblings), :exit reaps. THE STOP PATH (agreed with flow, 2026-11-16): the system process leads the process group and owns every child. Root ends with :stop (stdin EOF, a signal): the system process reaps root, ends its group (agents, test children) and exits. Root crashes or restarts on the same binary: the system process forks a new root and agents keep running, reconnecting to it on the one listener (:seon.server/listen-fd), resumed by its reconciler at their turn boundary (today an agent ends on its root socket's EOF; reconnect is runtime's and flow's). Root ends with :hand-over (a candidate binary takes over): the old system process ends its group and exits, freeing the writer and the listener for the new one. (5) stdin stays the system process's: root inherits fd 0 and reads its EOF as today, so bin/system stop is unchanged. DELETES: seon.fork-server/fork-server (root no longer forks), the standby and promote (the fork point is the parent: its death is the system's), boot!'s program load, instrument and warm in root (inherited). BOUND per docs/cost.md: the fork of root and of each agent ~5-10 ms; an agent's own memory <=5 MB after fork, after a collection, after one read; the system process loads O(forms moved) per notice. PROOFS, red first: seon.main-test/root-is-the-system-processs-first-fork (root's parent pid is the binary's main, and agents are root's siblings), and the fresh agent's private bytes row. Then trace the start-up writes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
